###  How to Mock Backend Functionality in Your React Component with MSW

###  API Endpoint & Mocking Guide (MSW)
This guide explains how to:

- Define a new API endpoint
- Describe request and response schemas
- Register the endpoint in OpenAPI structure
- Mock the backend with MSW (Mock Service Worker)
- Connect the MSW mock to your app

We’ll walk through mocking the **sign-up** flow, but this applies to any resource (/plans, /login, etc.).

- Step 1 — Define Endpoint Schema
All schemas and endpoints are defined in YAML under `apps/frontend/src/libs/modules/api/schema/main.yaml`:

Example:

```
paths:
  /auth/register:
    post:
      $ref: "./endpoints/auth.yaml#/register"
```

1.1 Create Request/Response Schemas
Add or extend schema objects in:

- Step 1.1: Use fetch in Your Component `apps/frontend/src/libs/modules/api/schema/endpoints/auth.yaml`

Example:

```
schemas:
  User:
    type: object
    required:
      - id
      - email
      - name
    properties:
      id:
        type: string
      email:
        type: string
        format: email
      name:
        type: string

  UserSignUpRequestDto:
    type: object
    required:
      - email
      - name
      - password
    properties:
      email:
        type: string
        format: email
      name:
        type: string
      password:
        type: string
        format: password

  AuthResponse:
    type: object
    required:
      - token
      - user
    properties:
      token:
        type: string
      user:
        $ref: '#/schemas/User'
```

- Step 2 — Define the Endpoint
In the same auth.yaml, define the endpoint:

```
register:
  summary: Sign up a new user
  requestBody:
    required: true
    content:
      application/json:
        schema:
          $ref: '#/schemas/UserSignUpRequestDto'
  responses:
    '201':
      description: Successful operation
      content:
        application/json:
          schema:
            $ref: '#/schemas/AuthResponse'
    '400':
      $ref: '../shared/responses.yaml#/BadRequestError'
```

If you need shared errors:

```
# apps/frontend/src/libs/modules/api/schema/shared/responses.yaml

BadRequestError:
  description: Bad request
  content:
    application/json:
      schema:
        $ref: "#/schemas/Error"
```

- Step 3 — Register the Endpoint in OpenAPI Paths
Add your endpoint to the OpenAPI spec:

```
# apps/frontend/src/libs/modules/api/schema/main.yaml

paths:
  /auth/register:
    post:
      $ref: "./endpoints/auth.yaml#/register"
```

run command `npm run api` - it will make your endpoints fully type safe, with all the benefits of the typescript while creating mock handlers

In our project, we don’t call fetch() directly in components. Instead, we use:
createAsyncThunk for async logic
authApi.signUp() for request abstraction
dispatch(authActions.signUp(payload)) in the component
So we mock backend calls by intercepting actual requests made from authApi.

- Step 4 — Trigger the API in the App

```
// apps/frontend/src/pages/auth/auth.tsx

const handleSignUpSubmit = useCallback(
  (payload: UserSignUpRequestDto): void => {
    void dispatch(authActions.signUp(payload));
  },
  [dispatch],
);
```

This triggers the thunk:

```
// apps/frontend/src/modules/auth/slices/actions.ts

export const signUp = createAsyncThunk<
  UserDto,
  UserSignUpRequestDto,
  AsyncThunkConfig
>(`${sliceName}/sign-up`, async (registerPayload, { extra }) => {
  const { authApi, storage } = extra;

  const { token, user } = await authApi.signUp(registerPayload);
  await storage.set(StorageKey.TOKEN, token);

  return user;
});
```

Which internally calls:

```
// apps/frontend/src/modules/auth/auth-api.ts

public async signUp(payload: UserSignUpRequestDto): Promise<UserSignUpResponseDto> {
  const response = await this.load(
    this.getFullEndpoint(AuthApiPath.SIGN_UP, {}),
    {
      contentType: ContentType.JSON,
      hasAuth: false,
      method: HTTPRequestMethod.POST,
      payload: JSON.stringify(payload),
    },
  );

  return await response.json<UserSignUpResponseDto>();
}
```

so MSW should mock this POST /register request initiated inside authApi.signUp().

Make sure the URL matches the backend route exactly — in this case: **/register**

- Step 5: Create a Mock Handler for That Endpoint

This simulates the backend’s behavior.
Create a new file:

`apps/frontend/src/modules/auth/mocks/handlers/sign-up-handlers.ts`

sign-up-handlers.ts:

```
import { delay, HttpResponse } from "msw";
import { http } from "~/libs/modules/api/mocks/http.js";
import { ApiSchemas } from "~/libs/modules/api/schema/schema.js";

const mockUsers: ApiSchemas["User"][] = [
	{
		id: "1",
		email: "admin@gmail.com",
		name: "admin",
	},
];

const generateTokens = async (payload: { userId: string; email: string }) =>
	"your.defined.token";

const userPasswords = new Map<string, string>();
